        '1. THEOPHILUS: Luke addressed his two-volume history to ''most excellent Theophilus'' so that he might have certainty concerning the things he had been taught. (Luke 1:3; Acts 1:1) 2. BELSHAZZAR: Belshazzar held a blasphemous banquet for a thousand of his lords; Daniel interpreted MENE, MENE, TEKEL, UPHARSIN, and the king was slain that night. (Daniel 5:1-30) 3. NICODEMUS: Nicodemus was taught about being born of the Spirit, defended Jesus before the Pharisees, and assisted Joseph of Arimathea in embalming Christ''s body. (John 3:1-12; John 19:39) 4. GAMALIEL: Gamaliel, leader of the School of Hillel, advised the Sanhedrin to leave the apostles alone, cautioning that opposing God''s work is impossible. (Acts 5:34-40; Acts 22:3) 5. JETHRO: Jethro priest of Midian brought Moses'' wife and sons to Sinai and counseled him to delegate smaller judicial cases to capable, God-fearing men. (Exodus 18:1-24) 6. DERBE: Paul fled to Derbe, preached the gospel, and made many disciples; Gaius of Derbe later accompanied Paul on his final journey to Jerusalem. (Acts 14:20-21; Acts 20:4) 7. LYSTRA: In Lystra, crowds tried to offer sacrifices to Barnabas and Paul, then stoned Paul and left him for dead; Lystra was Timothy''s hometown. (Acts 14:8-20; Acts 16:1-2) 8. SHUSHAN: Shushan (Susa) was the winter capital of the Persian Empire where King Ahasuerus sat on his royal throne and held his six-month banquet. (Esther 1:2; Nehemiah 1:1; Daniel 8:2) 9. SUCCOTH: The Israelites journeyed from Rameses to Succoth (''Booths''), about six hundred thousand men on foot, besides women and children. (Exodus 12:37; Exodus 13:20) 10. CYRENE: Cyrene was an ancient Greek and Roman city in modern-day Libya; Simon of Cyrene was the father of Alexander and Rufus. (Matthew 27:32; Mark 15:21)',
