        '1. ZAPHENATHPANEAH: What Egyptian title of honor did Pharaoh bestow upon Joseph when appointing him prime minister over the granaries of Egypt? (Genesis 41:45) 2. TIRZAH: One of the five inheritance-seeking daughters of Zelophehad, also early capital of northern Israel (Numbers 27:1; 1 Kings 14:17) 3. AKELDAMA: Aramaic name meaning "Field of Blood", bought with the thirty pieces of silver (Acts 1:19; Matthew 27:8) 4. ENGEDI: Oasis and crag of wild goats by the Dead Sea where David cut off the corner of Saul''s robe in a cave (1 Samuel 24:1-4) 5. ZIKLAG: Philistine border city given to David by King Achish, raided and burned by Amalekites (1 Samuel 27:6; 30:1) 6. JEZREEL: Fertile valley and royal winter city where Naboth kept a vineyard coveted by Ahab (1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4) 7. MIZPAH: Watchtower town where Israel assembled before Samuel and where Governor Gedaliah governed (1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23) 8. GIBEON: City whose inhabitants tricked Joshua with moldy bread, where the sun stood still in battle (Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12) 9. AIJALON: Valley over which Joshua commanded the moon to stand motionless during the fight for Gibeon (Joshua 10:12-13) 10. SHILOH: Early central sanctuary in Ephraim where the Tabernacle stood and young Samuel ministered (Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21)',
