        '"TIRZAH","ZIKLAG","JEZREEL","MIZPAH","SHILOH","TROAS","MILETUS","LYSTRA","ICONIUM","PAPHOS"'::text[],
        'TIRZAH|ZIKLAG|JEZREEL|MIZPAH|SHILOH|TROAS|MILETUS|LYSTRA|ICONIUM|PAPHOS',
        '1. TIRZAH: One of the five inheritance-seeking daughters of Zelophehad, also early capital of northern Israel (Numbers 27:1; 1 Kings 14:17) 2. ZIKLAG: Philistine border city given to David by King Achish, raided and burned by Amalekites (1 Samuel 27:6; 30:1) 3. JEZREEL: Fertile valley and royal winter city where Naboth kept a vineyard coveted by Ahab (1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4) 4. MIZPAH: Watchtower town where Israel assembled before Samuel and where Governor Gedaliah governed (1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23) 5. SHILOH: Early central sanctuary in Ephraim where the Tabernacle stood and young Samuel ministered (Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21) 6. TROAS: Coastal city in Mysia where Paul saw the night vision of the man of Macedonia pleading "Come over and help us" (Acts 16:8-10) 7. MILETUS: Port city where Paul gave his poignant farewell address to the elders of the church of Ephesus (Acts 20:17-38) 8. LYSTRA: City where Paul healed a crippled man, was hailed as Hermes, and was subsequently stoned and left for dead (Acts 14:8-19) 9. ICONIUM: City in Asia Minor where a great multitude of Jews and Greeks believed despite severe opposition (Acts 14:1-5) 10. PAPHOS: City on Cyprus where proconsul Sergius Paulus was converted after Elymas was struck blind (Acts 13:6-12)',
        'Numbers 27:1; 1 Kings 14:17; 1 Samuel 27:6; 30:1; 1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4; 1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23; Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21; Acts 16:8-10; Acts 20:17-38; Acts 14:8-19; Acts 14:1-5; Acts 13:6-12',
