        '1. SHILOH: Early central sanctuary in Ephraim where the Tabernacle stood and young Samuel ministered (Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21) 2. AIJALON: Valley over which Joshua commanded the moon to stand motionless during the fight for Gibeon (Joshua 10:12-13) 3. GIBEON: City whose inhabitants tricked Joshua with moldy bread, where the sun stood still in battle (Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12) 4. MIZPAH: Watchtower town where Israel assembled before Samuel and where Governor Gedaliah governed (1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23) 5. JEZREEL: Fertile valley and royal winter city where Naboth kept a vineyard coveted by Ahab (1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4) 6. ENGEDI: Oasis and crag of wild goats by the Dead Sea where David cut off the corner of Saul''s robe in a cave (1 Samuel 24:1-4) 7. EBENEZER: Memorial stone set up by Samuel between Mizpah and Shen saying "Thus far the LORD has helped us" (1 Samuel 7:12) 8. GESHEM: Arab leader who joined Sanballat and Tobiah in falsely accusing Nehemiah of planning rebellion (Nehemiah 2:19; 6:1-6) 9. TOBIAH: Ammonite official who sneered that even a fox walking on Nehemiah''s stone wall would break it down (Nehemiah 2:10; 4:3) 10. HARBONA: Royal eunuch who pointed out to King Ahasuerus the gallows Haman had prepared for Mordecai (Esther 7:9)',
