        '1. SHILOH: Early central sanctuary in Ephraim where the Tabernacle stood and young Samuel ministered (Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21) 2. ZIKLAG: Philistine border city given to David by King Achish, raided and burned by Amalekites (1 Samuel 27:6; 30:1) 3. SHIBBOLETH: Password word used by Jephthah''s men at Jordan crossings to detect Ephraimites who pronounced it Sibboleth (Judges 12:6) 4. TOBIAH: Ammonite official who sneered that even a fox walking on Nehemiah''s stone wall would break it down (Nehemiah 2:10; 4:3) 5. SANBALLAT: Horonite governor who conspired with Tobiah to mock and oppose the rebuilding of Jerusalem''s walls (Nehemiah 2:19; 4:1-8) 6. ZERESH: Wife of Haman who advised building a seventy-five-foot gallows to hang Mordecai (Esther 5:14; 6:13) 7. VASHTI: Queen of Persia who refused King Ahasuerus'' command to display her beauty before banquet guests (Esther 1:10-12) 8. EVILMERODACH: King of Babylon who released Judean King Jehoiachin from prison and gave him a regular allowance (2 Kings 25:27-30) 9. NEBUZARADAN: Captain of the Babylonian royal bodyguard who burned the Temple and broke down Jerusalem''s walls (2 Kings 25:8-11) 10. RABSHAKEH: Assyrian military envoy who shouted blasphemous intimidation in Hebrew on Jerusalem''s wall (2 Kings 18:17-35)',
