        '"AIJALON","AMASA","BEREA","GIBEON","ASAHEL","ABISHAI","ABNER","ABDON","JEZREEL","ENGEDI"'::text[],
        'AIJALON|AMASA|BEREA|GIBEON|ASAHEL|ABISHAI|ABNER|ABDON|JEZREEL|ENGEDI',
        '1. AIJALON: Valley over which Joshua commanded the moon to stand motionless during the fight for Gibeon (Joshua 10:12-13) 2. AMASA: Absalom''s army commander whom Joab deceitfully kissed and stabbed at the great rock of Gibeon (2 Samuel 20:9-10) 3. BEREA: The place whose people examined the Scriptures daily (Acts 17:10-12) 4. GIBEON: City whose inhabitants tricked Joshua with moldy bread, where the sun stood still in battle (Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12) 5. ASAHEL: Brother of Joab, as fleet of foot as a gazelle, slain by Abner''s reversed spear (2 Samuel 2:18-23) 6. ABISHAI: David''s fiercely loyal nephew who slipped with him into Saul''s camp and took the spear and water jug (1 Samuel 26:6-12) 7. ABNER: Commander of Saul''s army who made Ishbosheth king before defecting to David (2 Samuel 2:8; 3:12) 8. ABDON: Judge of Pirathon who had forty sons and thirty grandsons who rode on seventy donkeys (Judges 12:13-15) 9. JEZREEL: Fertile valley and royal winter city where Naboth kept a vineyard coveted by Ahab (1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4) 10. ENGEDI: Oasis and crag of wild goats by the Dead Sea where David cut off the corner of Saul''s robe in a cave (1 Samuel 24:1-4)',
        'Joshua 10:12-13; 2 Samuel 20:9-10; Acts 17:10-12; Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12; 2 Samuel 2:18-23; 1 Samuel 26:6-12; 2 Samuel 2:8; 3:12; Judges 12:13-15; 1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4; 1 Samuel 24:1-4',
