        '"SHILOH","AIJALON","GIBEON","MIZPAH","JEZREEL","ENGEDI","EBENEZER","GESHEM","ITTAI","AMASA"'::text[],
        'SHILOH|AIJALON|GIBEON|MIZPAH|JEZREEL|ENGEDI|EBENEZER|GESHEM|ITTAI|AMASA',
        '1. SHILOH: Early central sanctuary in Ephraim where the Tabernacle stood and young Samuel ministered (Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21) 2. AIJALON: Valley over which Joshua commanded the moon to stand motionless during the fight for Gibeon (Joshua 10:12-13) 3. GIBEON: City whose inhabitants tricked Joshua with moldy bread, where the sun stood still in battle (Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12) 4. MIZPAH: Watchtower town where Israel assembled before Samuel and where Governor Gedaliah governed (1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23) 5. JEZREEL: Fertile valley and royal winter city where Naboth kept a vineyard coveted by Ahab (1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4) 6. ENGEDI: Oasis and crag of wild goats by the Dead Sea where David cut off the corner of Saul''s robe in a cave (1 Samuel 24:1-4) 7. EBENEZER: Memorial stone set up by Samuel between Mizpah and Shen saying "Thus far the LORD has helped us" (1 Samuel 7:12) 8. GESHEM: Arab leader who joined Sanballat and Tobiah in falsely accusing Nehemiah of planning rebellion (Nehemiah 2:19; 6:1-6) 9. ITTAI: Loyal Philistine warrior of Gath who pledged allegiance to follow David even in exile (2 Samuel 15:19-22) 10. AMASA: Absalom''s army commander whom Joab deceitfully kissed and stabbed at the great rock of Gibeon (2 Samuel 20:9-10)',
        'Joshua 18:1; 1 Samuel 3:21; Joshua 10:12-13; Joshua 9:3-15; 10:12; 1 Samuel 7:5-6; 2 Kings 25:23; 1 Kings 21:1; Hosea 1:4; 1 Samuel 24:1-4; 1 Samuel 7:12; Nehemiah 2:19; 6:1-6; 2 Samuel 15:19-22; 2 Samuel 20:9-10',
