        'ARTEMAS|LAODICEA|PAPHOS|ICONIUM|LYSTRA|DERBE|CENCHREAE|BEREA|EUBULUS|LINUS',
        '1. ARTEMAS: Co-worker Paul considered sending to Titus in Crete before calling Titus to Nicopolis (Titus 3:12) 2. LAODICEA: Wealthy city whose lukewarm church was warned that Christ would spit them out of His mouth (Revelation 3:14-17) 3. PAPHOS: City on Cyprus where proconsul Sergius Paulus was converted after Elymas was struck blind (Acts 13:6-12) 4. ICONIUM: City in Asia Minor where a great multitude of Jews and Greeks believed despite severe opposition (Acts 14:1-5) 5. LYSTRA: City where Paul healed a crippled man, was hailed as Hermes, and was subsequently stoned and left for dead (Acts 14:8-19) 6. DERBE: Galatian city where Paul and Barnabas preached and made many disciples after fleeing Lystra (Acts 14:20-21) 7. CENCHREAE: Port of Corinth where Paul had his hair cut because of a vow, and home of deaconess Phoebe (Acts 18:18; Romans 16:1) 8. BEREA: Macedonian city whose noble citizens examined the Scriptures daily to verify Paul''s teachings (Acts 17:10-12) 9. EUBULUS: Brother in Rome who sent personal greetings to Timothy during Paul''s second Roman imprisonment (2 Timothy 4:21) 10. LINUS: Roman Christian mentioned in 2 Timothy, recognized early in church history as an elder of Rome (2 Timothy 4:21)',
        'Titus 3:12; Revelation 3:14-17; Acts 13:6-12; Acts 14:1-5; Acts 14:8-19; Acts 14:20-21; Acts 18:18; Romans 16:1; Acts 17:10-12; 2 Timothy 4:21',
