(
        'bible-puzzles-l4-c2',
        'bible-puzzles',
        4,
        2,
        'SPOT THE BIBLICAL MISTAKE:
"Three kings visited the newborn baby Jesus while He was lying in the manger in the stable at Bethlehem."',
        '"The visitors were Roman centurions, not eastern travelers","Scripture calls them Wise Men (Magi), does not specify their number as three, and they found Jesus in a house","Jesus was born in Nazareth, not Bethlehem of Judea","The gifts were copper, silver, and precious stones, not gold and frankincense"'::text[],
        'Scripture calls them Wise Men (Magi), does not specify their number as three, and they found Jesus in a house',
        'Matthew 2 calls them "wise men from the east" (not kings) and never specifies their number (tradition assumed three from the three gifts). Furthermore, Matthew 2:11 states: "when they were come into the house, they saw the young child."',
        'Matthew 2:1, 11',
        '{"puzzle_type":"Spot the Biblical Mistake","clues":["Read Matthew 2:1–11 and Luke 2:7–16.","Notice whether Matthew calls them \"kings\" or gives a number.","Notice where the wise men actually found the young child."],"hint":"Popular Christmas tradition adds numbers and royal titles not found in Matthew 2.","stage":1,"category":"Puzzle Foundations","level_theme":"Spot the Mistake","difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l4-c3',
        'bible-puzzles',
        4,
        3,
        'SPOT THE BIBLICAL MISTAKE:
"In the Garden of Eden, the serpent tempted Eve with an apple, telling her that eating it would make her like God."',
        '"The forbidden tree was called the Tree of Life, not the Tree of Knowledge","The serpent spoke directly to Adam first, not to Eve","The Bible never names the fruit as an apple, describing it only as the \"fruit of the tree\"","Eve refused the fruit and only Adam tasted of the food"'::text[],
        'The Bible never names the fruit as an apple, describing it only as the "fruit of the tree"',
        'Genesis nowhere mentions an apple. The text speaks only of the "fruit of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil." The popular "apple" tradition arose from the Latin word malum (meaning both "evil" and "apple").',
        'Genesis 2:17; 3:3, 6',
        '{"puzzle_type":"Spot the Biblical Mistake","clues":["Read Genesis 3:1–6 carefully.","Look at the botanical description of the forbidden food in Genesis."],"hint":"Check the exact wording of Genesis 3:6: \"the fruit thereof.\"","stage":1,"category":"Puzzle Foundations","level_theme":"Spot the Mistake","difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l4-c4',
        'bible-puzzles',
        4,
        4,
        'SPOT THE BIBLICAL MISTAKE:
"Jonah was swallowed by a massive white whale that kept him in its belly for three days and three nights."',
        '"Jonah spent forty days inside the creature, not three days and three nights","The Hebrew text of Jonah 1:17 states the LORD prepared a \"great fish,\" not specifically a whale","Jonah was thrown into the Red Sea, not the Mediterranean Sea","Jonah died immediately and was only brought to life on the shore"'::text[],
        'The Hebrew text of Jonah 1:17 states the LORD prepared a "great fish," not specifically a whale',
        'Jonah 1:17 states: "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish (dag gadol) to swallow up Jonah." While Jesus used the Greek ketos in Matthew 12:40, the Old Testament narrative specifies a specially prepared great fish.',
        'Jonah 1:17; Matthew 12:40',
        '{"puzzle_type":"Spot the Biblical Mistake","clues":["Read Jonah 1:17 and Matthew 12:40.","Examine how the original Hebrew text in Jonah identifies the sea creature."],"hint":"Examine the exact translation of Jonah 1:17 in the Hebrew scriptures.","stage":1,"category":"Puzzle Foundations","level_theme":"Spot the Mistake","difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
