(
        'bible-puzzles-l47-c1',
        'bible-puzzles',
        47,
        1,
        'BIBLICAL PARADOXES:
Paul writes in Romans 3:28: "For we hold that one is justified by faith apart from works of the law." Yet James writes in James 2:24: "You see that a person is justified by works and not by faith alone." How do Scripture and theological context harmonize this apparent paradox?',
        '"James was correcting and repudiating Paul's gospel as false teaching","Paul speaks of the root of justification before God by faith; James speaks of the fruit/vindication of genuine faith before men by works","Paul was writing to Gentiles while James was writing to angels","Paul changed his mind in his later epistles and agreed that circumcision was required"'::text[],
        'Paul speaks of the root of justification before God by faith; James speaks of the fruit/vindication of genuine faith before men by works',
        'Paul combats legalism (seeking salvation by meritorious works), teaching that faith alone justifies before God. James combats antinomianism (barren, dead intellectual assent), proving that genuine justifying faith inevitably produces good works.',
        'Romans 3:28; 4:1–5; James 2:14–26',
        '{"puzzle_type":"The Bible Detective","clues":["Paul addresses how a sinner is declared righteous before God at salvation (root of salvation).","James addresses how a professing believer demonstrates living faith before people (fruit of salvation).","Both quote Genesis 15:6 regarding Abraham, but James shows Abraham''s faith was vindicated when he offered Isaac in Genesis 22."],"hint":"Paul deals with the root of faith before God; James deals with the visible fruit before men.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Biblical Paradoxes","difficulty":"hard","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l47-c2',
        'bible-puzzles',
        47,
        2,
        'BIBLICAL PARADOXES:
In Proverbs 26:4, Solomon writes: "Answer not a fool according to his folly, lest you be like him yourself." In the very next verse (Proverbs 26:5), Solomon writes: "Answer a fool according to his folly, lest he be wise in his own eyes." What is the profound practical wisdom harmonizing these twin proverbs?',
        '"The two verses were written by two opposing authors who argued in the temple","Do not adopt a fool's foolish arguments and temper, but do expose the absurdity of his premises to humble his conceit","King Solomon accidentally duplicated a scribal error that was never corrected","Verse 4 applies only on weekdays, while verse 5 applies on the Sabbath"'::text[],
        'Do not adopt a fool''s foolish arguments and temper, but do expose the absurdity of his premises to humble his conceit',
        'Proverbs 26:4–5 demonstrates situational discernment: never stoop to a fool''s low methods (anger, slander, irrationality) so you don''t become like him; yet apply his own logic to show its devastating flaws so he doesn''t remain wise in his own conceit.',
        'Proverbs 26:4–5',
        '{"puzzle_type":"The Bible Detective","clues":["Two consecutive verses offering seemingly opposite advice.","Verse 4 warns against adopting the fool''s abusive methods and irrational mindset.","Verse 5 commands exposing the fool''s false premise so his pride is dismantled."],"hint":"Never copy his foolish behavior, but do refute his flawed thinking.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Biblical Paradoxes","difficulty":"hard","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l47-c3',
        'bible-puzzles',
        47,
        3,
        'BIBLICAL PARADOXES:
In 2 Samuel 24:1, the text states: "Again the anger of the Lord was kindled against Israel, and he incited David against them, saying, ''Go, number Israel and Judah.''" Yet in 1 Chronicles 21:1, the exact same event is recorded: "Then Satan stood against Israel and incited David to number Israel." How does Scripture harmonize God''s sovereignty and Satan''s agency?',
        '"1 Chronicles was written by a Persian author who believed in two equal creator gods","God in His sovereign judgment permitted Satan as the instrument to tempt David's pride, similar to the testing of Job","David numbered the people twice over a forty-year period","Satan is merely a poetic Hebrew word for King Joab"'::text[],
        'God in His sovereign judgment permitted Satan as the instrument to tempt David''s pride, similar to the testing of Job',
        'Scripture shows God''s ultimate sovereignty working through secondary causes: God in His anger permitted Satan to tempt David, holding David fully responsible for his sinful pride in numbering his military forces.',
        '2 Samuel 24:1; 1 Chronicles 21:1; Job 1:12; 2:6',
        '{"puzzle_type":"The Bible Detective","clues":["God sovereignly permitted the trial to test and judge Israel.","Satan was the active malicious agent tempting David''s pride.","Similar to Job 1–2 where Satan acted as the adversary, but only within God''s sovereign permission."],"hint":"God permitted the trial sovereignly; Satan was the malicious agent provoking David.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Biblical Paradoxes","difficulty":"master","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l47-c4',
        'bible-puzzles',
        47,
        4,
        'BIBLICAL PARADOXES:
In Luke 14:26, Jesus states: "If anyone comes to me and does not hate his own father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters, yes, and even his own life, he cannot be my disciple." Yet Ephesians 6:2 commands: "Honor your father and mother," and 1 John 4:20 says whoever hates his brother is a liar. What does "hate" mean in Jesus'' Semitic idiom?',
        '"To abandon one's family in poverty and flee to the desert","To disown one's children and give all property to the Roman state","To harbor genuine bitterness and resentment against one's parents","To love less by comparison; allegiance to Christ must transcend all earthly family devotions"'::text[],
        'To love less by comparison; allegiance to Christ must transcend all earthly family devotions',
        'In biblical Hebrew/Aramaic idiom, "love" and "hate" are often used comparatively to express preference (as in Matthew 10:37: "Whoever loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me"). Our devotion to Christ must make all other loyalties pale in comparison.',
        'Luke 14:26; Matthew 10:37; Genesis 29:30–31; Romans 9:13',
        '{"puzzle_type":"The Bible Detective","clues":["A common biblical idiom of comparison and priority (as with Jacob and Esau in Malachi 1:3).","Loving someone less by comparison to supreme love for Christ.","No earthly affection can rival absolute allegiance to Jesus."],"hint":"Comparative love: loving Christ supremely so that other devotions are secondary.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Biblical Paradoxes","difficulty":"medium","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l47-c5',
        'bible-puzzles',
        47,
        5,
        'BIBLICAL PARADOXES:
In Galatians 6:2, Paul commands: "Bear one another''s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ." Yet only three verses later in Galatians 6:5, Paul states: "For each will have to bear his own load." What is the linguistic and practical resolution to this apparent contradiction?',
        '"Verse 2 was spoken by Peter while verse 5 was added by a Roman scribe","Paul was writing to two different factions in the Galatian church","It is an unresolvable contradiction showing Paul's frustration with the legalists","We must help others carry crushing crises (burdens), but each person must shoulder their own daily personal responsibility and accountability before God (load)"'::text[],
        'We must help others carry crushing crises (burdens), but each person must shoulder their own daily personal responsibility and accountability before God (load)',
        'In Galatians 6:2, ''bare'' refers to heavy crushing weights (grief, illness, temptation) where brothers must step in to help. In Galatians 6:5, ''phortion'' refers to an individual''s backpack (personal duty and moral accountability at the judgment seat).',
        'Galatians 6:2–5',
        '{"puzzle_type":"The Bible Detective","clues":["Two different Greek words are used in the original text.","Verse 2 uses ''bare'' (crushing, overwhelming weights that require communal help).","Verse 5 uses ''phortion'' (a personal soldier''s pack or individual moral accountability before God)."],"hint":"Help others with crushing weights; carry your own personal backpack of accountability.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Biblical Paradoxes","difficulty":"hard","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l48-c1',
        'bible-puzzles',
        48,
        1,
        'ULTIMATE LOGIC GRID:
Four Old Testament figures experienced extraordinary encounters with supernatural fire from heaven:
1. Moses on Mount Horeb
2. Elijah on Mount Carmel
3. Gideon at the rock of Ophrah
4. Solomon at the temple dedication
Match each to the exact manifestation of fire:',
        '"Moses: Burning bush; Elijah: Temple dedication; Gideon: Consumed altar; Solomon: Rock staff","Moses: Unconsumed burning bush; Elijah: Consumed waterlogged altar; Gideon: Fire out of rock by angel's staff; Solomon: Fire at temple dedication","Moses: Consumed altar; Elijah: Unconsumed bush; Gideon: Temple dedication; Solomon: Rock staff","Moses: Fire out of rock; Elijah: Burning bush; Gideon: Consumed altar; Solomon: Temple dedication"'::text[],
        'Moses: Unconsumed burning bush; Elijah: Consumed waterlogged altar; Gideon: Fire out of rock by angel''s staff; Solomon: Fire at temple dedication',
        'Moses witnessed the burning bush (Exodus 3). Gideon saw fire rise from the rock (Judges 6:21). Elijah called down fire consuming the wet sacrifice (1 Kings 18). Solomon saw heavenly fire consume the temple offerings (2 Chron 7:1).',
        'Exodus 3:2; 1 Kings 18:38; Judges 6:21; 2 Chronicles 7:1',
        '{"puzzle_type":"Bible Logic Grid","clues":["Moses: A bush that burned with fire yet was not consumed.","Elijah: Fire fell and consumed the water-drenched sacrifice, wood, stones, dust, and water.","Gideon: Fire sprang up out of the rock when the Angel of the Lord touched the meat and cakes with his staff.","Solomon: Fire came down from heaven and consumed the burnt offerings when he finished his dedicatory prayer."],"hint":"Moses = bush; Gideon = rock staff; Elijah = Carmel altar; Solomon = 2 Chronicles 7.","stage":10,"category":"The Ultimate Bible Puzzle","level_theme":"Ultimate Logic Grid","difficulty":"master","timerSeconds":20,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-puzzles-l48-c2',
        'bible-puzzles',
        48,
        2,
        'ULTIMATE LOGIC GRID:
Three major biblical covenants were accompanied by specific mountain encounters:
1. Abrahamic covenant confirmation
2. Mosaic covenant inauguration
3. Jesus'' New Covenant ratification and transfiguration
Match each covenant mediator to his sacred mountain:',
        '"Abraham: Mount Moriah; Moses: Mount Sinai (Horeb); Jesus: Mount of Transfiguration & Golgotha","Abraham: Mount Nebo; Moses: Mount Hermon; Jesus: Mount Sinai","Abraham: Mount Gerizim; Moses: Mount Ebal; Jesus: Mount Nebo","Abraham: Mount Sinai; Moses: Mount Moriah; Jesus: Mount Carmel"'::text[],
        'Abraham: Mount Moriah; Moses: Mount Sinai (Horeb); Jesus: Mount of Transfiguration & Golgotha',
        'Abraham journeyed to Mount Moriah (Genesis 22). Moses received the Law on Mount Sinai/Horeb (Exodus 19–20). Jesus was transfigured on the high mountain (Matthew 17) and completed the covenant at Golgotha.',
        'Genesis 22:2; Exodus 19:16–20; Matthew 17:1–2; John 19:17',
