        26,
        2,
        'WHO AM I?
I was the father of five daughters from the tribe of Manasseh who died in the wilderness during the forty years of wandering. Because I had no sons, my daughters boldly approached Moses, Eleazar the priest, the chiefs, and the congregation at the entrance of the tent of meeting, asking why my family name should disappear. God ruled in their favor, instituting a permanent legal statute that daughters inherit when a man has no sons. Who am I?',
        '"Hepher","Zelophehad","Machir","Gilead"'::text[],
        'Zelophehad',
        'Zelophehad of the tribe of Manasseh had five daughters whose historic legal appeal secured property inheritance rights for daughters.',
        'Numbers 27:1–11; 36:1–12',
        '{"riddle_type":"Who Am I?","is_typed":false,"clues":["Daughters: Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Tirzah.","Died for his own sins in the wilderness, not participating in Korah’s rebellion.","Case established female inheritance law in Israel (Numbers 27:1–11)."],"hint":"Father of five daughters whose legal appeal established female inheritance rights.","testament":"Old Testament","acceptedAlternatives":["Zelophehad of Manasseh","Salphad"],"stage":6,"stage_title":"Forgotten Names","level_theme":"Forgotten Pentateuch & History Names","difficulty":"medium","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
