During a decadent feast for a thousand of his lords in Babylon, this co-regent king drank wine from the sacred gold and silver vessels looted from the Jerusalem Temple while praising gods of gold, silver, bronze, iron, wood, and stone. Suddenly, the fingers of a human hand appeared and wrote four mysterious Aramaic words on the plaster of the palace wall opposite the lampstand: MENE, MENE, TEKEL, UPHARSIN. His face turned pale, his thoughts terrified him, his hip joints shook, and his knees knocked against each other. Who was this Babylonian king slain that very night when Darius the Mede took the city?',
