        'bible-riddles',
        35,
        3,
        'WHAT AM I?
In Zechariah 11, the prophet acts as a shepherd of the flock doomed to slaughter and takes two shepherd’s staffs to pasture them. He names one staff "Favor" (or Beauty) and the other staff "Union" (or Binds). Later, when the prophet detests the flock and breaks the first staff, it annuls the covenant with all peoples; when he breaks the second staff, it shatters the brotherly covenant between Judah and Israel. What were the names of these two prophetic staffs?',
        '"Justice and Peace","Mercy and Truth","Favor and Union","Rod and Staff"'::text[],
        'Favor and Union',
        'Zechariah named his two shepherd’s staffs Favor (Beauty) and Union (Bands), breaking both to symbolize broken divine covenants and fraternal unity.',
        'Zechariah 11:7–14',
        '{"riddle_type":"What Am I?","is_typed":false,"clues":["Zechariah 11:7, 10, 14.","Staff 1: Favor (Beauty).","Staff 2: Union (Bands / Binds)."],"hint":"Two symbolic shepherd’s staffs in Zechariah 11 broken to depict broken covenants.","testament":"Old Testament","acceptedAlternatives":["Beauty and Bands","Favor & Union","Beauty and Union","Grace and Unity"],"stage":7,"stage_title":"The Difficult Riddles","level_theme":"Forensic Prophetic Dramas","difficulty":"master","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-riddles-l35-c4',
        'bible-riddles',
        35,
        4,
        'THE EVENT RIDDLE:
In Ezekiel 5, the priest-prophet is commanded to take a sharp sword and use it as a barber’s razor on his own head and beard. He must weigh the shorn hair in balances and divide it into three equal portions: burn one third in the center of the city, strike one third with the sword all around the city, and scatter one third to the wind, saving only a small fraction tucked into the hem of his robe. What did this dramatic hair-shaving ritual dramatize?',
        '"The Threefold Fate of Jerusalem’s Citizens","The Consecration of the Aaronic Priesthood","The Nazirite Cleansing Ceremony","The Cleansing of the Leper"'::text[],
        'The Threefold Fate of Jerusalem’s Citizens',
        'Ezekiel’s divided beard hair symbolized the threefold judgment on Jerusalem: pestilence/famine, the sword, and exile among the nations.',
        'Ezekiel 5:1–12',
        '{"riddle_type":"The Event Riddle","is_typed":false,"clues":["One third dying of pestilence and famine in the siege of Jerusalem.","One third falling by the sword around the city.","One third scattered into exile among the nations."],"hint":"Ezekiel’s razor prophecy dividing his beard into thirds to depict Jerusalem’s destruction.","testament":"Old Testament","acceptedAlternatives":["Fate of Jerusalem","Threefold Fate of Jerusalem","Destruction of Jerusalem","Ezekiel’s Hair Prophecy"],"stage":7,"stage_title":"The Difficult Riddles","level_theme":"Forensic Prophetic Dramas","difficulty":"hard","timerSeconds":30,"curated":true}'::jsonb
