        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Broken Ministry & Succession","chain_type":"wrong_chain","chain_nodes":["James","Peter","John","Judas Iscariot [BROKEN]"],"broken_link_index":3,"is_typed":true,"acceptedAlternatives":["Judas","Jude the apostle","Judas the brother of James"],"difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":45,"points":100,"testament":"New Testament","hint1":"Four-letter English name of the author of the 25-verse epistle.","hint2":"Jude 1:1: \"Jude, the servant of Jesus Christ, and brother of James.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l39-c4',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        39,
        4,
        'BROKEN CHAIN DETECTIVE: Examine this United Monarchy royal line:
Saul (40 years) → David (40 years) → Solomon (40 years) → Jeroboam son of Nebat (over all twelve tribes)
Why does Jeroboam break the chain of kings over a united Israel?',
        '"Jeroboam never became a king","Jeroboam was a priest","Jeroboam was killed by Goliath","Jeroboam rebelled and reigned only over the ten northern tribes; Rehoboam succeeded Solomon"'::text[],
        'Jeroboam rebelled and reigned only over the ten northern tribes; Rehoboam succeeded Solomon',
        'Solomon’s immediate successor was his son Rehoboam (1 Kings 11:43). Jeroboam son of Nebat led the rebellion of the ten northern tribes, creating the divided northern kingdom of Israel with golden calves at Bethel and Dan (1 Kings 12:1-20, 26-30). Jeroboam is the broken link.',
        '1 Kings 11:43; 12:1-20, 26-30',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Broken Ministry & Succession","chain_type":"wrong_chain","chain_nodes":["Saul","David","Solomon","Jeroboam over all 12 tribes [BROKEN]"],"broken_link_index":3,"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":45,"points":100,"testament":"Old Testament","hint1":"Solomon’s son was Rehoboam, whose harsh answer divided the kingdom.","hint2":"1 Kings 12:16: \"What portion have we in David? neither have we inheritance in the son of Jesse.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l39-c5',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        39,
        5,
        'BROKEN CHAIN DETECTIVE: Examine this Major Prophets chronological chain:
Isaiah (Hezekiah’s reign) → Jeremiah (Fall of Jerusalem) → Ezekiel (Chebar river in Babylon) → Daniel (Babylon & Persia) → Jonah
Why does Jonah break this Major Prophets sequence?',
        '"Jonah was a king of Moab","Jonah never wrote a book","Jonah lived after Malachi","Jonah was an 8th-century Minor Prophet to Nineveh, centuries before Daniel and Ezekiel"'::text[],
        'Jonah was an 8th-century Minor Prophet to Nineveh, centuries before Daniel and Ezekiel',
        'Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, and Daniel constitute the four Major Prophets in chronological order surrounding the exile. Jonah was a Minor Prophet who prophesied during the reign of Jeroboam II of Israel in the 8th century BC (2 Kings 14:25). Jonah is the broken link!',
        '2 Kings 14:25; Jonah 1:1; Daniel 1:1',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Broken Ministry & Succession","chain_type":"wrong_chain","chain_nodes":["Isaiah","Jeremiah","Ezekiel","Daniel","Jonah [BROKEN]"],"broken_link_index":4,"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Moderate","timerSeconds":45,"points":100,"testament":"Old Testament","hint1":"Jonah ministered in Nineveh in 2 Kings 14, long before the Babylonian captivity.","hint2":"2 Kings 14:25: \"according to the word of the LORD God of Israel, which he spake by the hand of his servant Jonah.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l40-c1',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        40,
        1,
        'DEEP SCRIPTURE STUDY: In Matthew 1:8, compare the royal genealogy with 1 Chronicles 3:11-12:
Matthew 1:8: "and Joram begat Ozias (Uzziah)."
1 Chronicles 3:11-12 reveals that THREE consecutive kings of Judah were intentionally omitted between Joram and Uzziah because of Athaliah’s idolatrous curse. Name these three omitted kings:',
        '"Hezekiah, Manasseh, and Amon","Jehoahaz, Jehoiakim, and Zedekiah","Ahaziah, Joash, and Amaziah","Asa, Jehoshaphat, and Joram"'::text[],
        'Ahaziah, Joash, and Amaziah',
        '1 Chronicles 3:11-12 records: "Joram his son, Ahaziah his son, Joash his son, Amaziah his son, Azariah (Uzziah) his son." Matthew deliberately compresses the royal line into three 14-generation periods, omitting Ahaziah, Joash, and Amaziah (the three generations descended from Ahab and Jezebel’s daughter Athaliah).',
        'Matthew 1:8; 1 Chronicles 3:11-12; 2 Kings 8:25; 11:2; 14:1',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Deep Scripture Study: Forensic Genealogies","chain_type":"wrong_chain","chain_nodes":["Joram","Omitted generation 1","Omitted generation 2","Omitted generation 3","Uzziah"],"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Difficult","timerSeconds":90,"points":150,"testament":"New Testament","dossier":"Matthew 1:8 omits three kings of Judah to structure the genealogy into three sets of 14 generations.","study_mode":true,"hint1":"Slain by Jehu, hidden in the temple as a baby, and assassinated at Lachish.","hint2":"1 Chronicles 3:11-12: \"Ahaziah his son, Joash his son, Amaziah his son.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l40-c2',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        40,
        2,
        'DEEP SCRIPTURE STUDY: In Jeremiah 22:24-30, God pronounced a permanent curse upon King Coniah (Jeconiah / Jehoiachin): "Write ye this man childless, a man that shall not prosper in his days: for no man of his seed shall prosper, sitting upon the throne of David." How does the genealogy in Luke 3 bypass this blood curse while preserving Christ’s legal right to David’s throne?',
        '"Luke traces Jesus through David’s son Nathan, rather than Solomon and Jeconiah","Luke traces Jesus through King Saul","Luke skips David completely","Luke traces Jesus through Aaron the high priest"'::text[],
        'Luke traces Jesus through David’s son Nathan, rather than Solomon and Jeconiah',
        'Matthew 1 traces Joseph’s royal legal line through Solomon and Jeconiah (establishing the royal title). Luke 3:31 traces Mary’s physical bloodline through Nathan the son of David ("which was the son of Nathan, which was the son of David"), completely bypassing Jeconiah’s blood curse in Jeremiah 22:30!',
        'Jeremiah 22:28-30; Matthew 1:11-12; Luke 3:23-31',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Deep Scripture Study: Forensic Genealogies","chain_type":"old_to_new","chain_nodes":["Jeremiah 22:30 (Coniah blood curse)","Solomonic royal line in Matthew 1","Luke 3 genealogy","?"],"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Difficult","timerSeconds":90,"points":150,"testament":"New Testament","dossier":"Jeremiah 22:30: \"for no man of his seed shall prosper, sitting upon the throne of David, and ruling any more in Judah.\"","study_mode":true,"hint1":"David’s son Nathan, not the prophet Nathan.","hint2":"Luke 3:31: \"which was the son of Nathan, which was the son of David.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l40-c3',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        40,
        3,
        'Type the name of the missing father in Luke 3:35-36 who appears in the Septuagint/Luke genealogy between Arphaxad and Sala, but is absent from the Hebrew Masoretic Genesis 11 text:',
        '{}'::text[],
        'Cainan',
        'Luke 3:35-36 records: "which was the son of Sala, which was the son of Cainan, which was the son of Arphaxad." This second Cainan appears in the Greek Septuagint genealogy of Genesis 11:12, while the Hebrew Masoretic text moves directly from Arphaxad to Salah.',
        'Genesis 11:12 (LXX); Luke 3:35-36',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Deep Scripture Study: Forensic Genealogies","chain_type":"missing_link","chain_nodes":["Arphaxad","?","Sala","Heber"],"is_typed":true,"acceptedAlternatives":["Kainan"],"difficulty":"Difficult","timerSeconds":75,"points":150,"testament":"New Testament","hint1":"Six-letter name beginning with C (spelled with K in Greek).","hint2":"Luke 3:36: \"which was the son of Cainan, which was the son of Arphaxad.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l40-c4',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        40,
        4,
        'DEEP SCRIPTURE STUDY: In 1 Chronicles 2:13-15, David is listed as the SEVENTH son of Jesse. But in 1 Samuel 16:10-11 and 17:12, how many sons did Jesse have when Samuel visited Bethlehem?',
        '"Twelve sons","Seven sons","Ten sons","Eight sons (one died without issue before the Chronicles records)"'::text[],
        'Eight sons (one died without issue before the Chronicles records)',
        '1 Samuel 16:10 and 17:12 state that Jesse had eight sons ("and Jesse had eight sons: and the man went among men for an old man in the days of Saul"). In 1 Chronicles 2:13-15, only seven are numbered (Eliab, Abinadab, Shimma, Nethaneel, Raddai, Ozem, and David the seventh) because one son died childless, leaving no lineage for the post-exilic tribal registry.',
        '1 Samuel 16:10-11; 17:12; 1 Chronicles 2:13-15',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Deep Scripture Study: Forensic Genealogies","chain_type":"wrong_chain","chain_nodes":["1 Samuel 16 (Eight sons)","Seven pass before Samuel","David fetched from field","1 Chronicles 2:15 numbers David 7th","?"],"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Difficult","timerSeconds":90,"points":150,"testament":"Old Testament","dossier":"1 Samuel 17:12: \"Now David was the son of that Ephrathite of Beth-lehem-judah, whose name was Jesse; and he had eight sons.\"","study_mode":true,"hint1":"Jesse originally had eight sons in the narrative of 1 Samuel 16–17.","hint2":"1 Samuel 17:12: \"and he had eight sons.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      ),
(
        'bible-chain-challenge-l40-c5',
        'bible-chain-challenge',
        40,
        5,
        'DEEP SCRIPTURE STUDY: In Genesis 46:27, the souls of the house of Jacob which came into Egypt are counted as threescore and ten (70). Yet in Acts 7:14, Stephen testifies that Joseph called his father Jacob and all his kindred, "threescore and fifteen (75) souls." Why does Stephen state 75 souls?',
        '"Stephen quoted the Greek Septuagint (LXX), which added five grandsons/great-grandsons of Joseph","Stephen made an arithmetic error","Stephen counted five Egyptian servants","Stephen included Moses and Aaron"'::text[],
        'Stephen quoted the Greek Septuagint (LXX), which added five grandsons/great-grandsons of Joseph',
        'Genesis 46:27 in the Hebrew Masoretic text numbers 70 souls. However, the Septuagint (LXX) version of Genesis 46:20-27 and Exodus 1:5 explicitly adds the sons and grandsons of Manasseh and Ephraim (Machir, Gilead, Shuthelah, Tahath, and Eden), totaling 75. Stephen, a Hellenistic deacon, accurately quoted the inspired Septuagint text in common Greek circulation!',
        'Genesis 46:27; Exodus 1:5 (LXX); Acts 7:14',
        '{"stage":8,"stage_title":"The Broken Chain","level_title":"Deep Scripture Study: Forensic Genealogies","chain_type":"old_to_new","chain_nodes":["Genesis 46:27 (Hebrew 70)","Septuagint adds Joseph’s descendants","Stephen’s speech in Acts 7:14","?"],"is_typed":false,"difficulty":"Difficult","timerSeconds":90,"points":150,"testament":"New Testament","dossier":"Acts 7:14: \"Then sent Joseph, and called his father Jacob to him, and all his kindred, threescore and fifteen souls.\"","study_mode":true,"hint1":"The Greek translation of the Old Testament used by early Christians.","hint2":"Acts 7:14: \"threescore and fifteen souls.\"","curated":true}'::jsonb
      )
